{"severity":"error","file":"errors/deterministic_violation_1.march","start_line":22,"start_col":2,"end_line":22,"end_col":16,"message":"`mod LeakyDet` (declared `cap deterministic`) declares `needs IO.Clock`, which admits the clock, a nondeterminism source, directly or through a stdlib wrapper such as `DateTime.now`.\n\nDeclare only capabilities that are neither the clock nor an RNG (`IO.Console`, `IO.FileRead`, ...), and move the code that reads the clock or an RNG to a module without `cap deterministic`.","code":"deterministic_violation","fix":null,"labels":[],"notes":[],"category":"CONTRACT"}
{"severity":"error","file":"errors/deterministic_violation_1.march","start_line":26,"start_col":4,"end_line":26,"end_col":16,"message":"`ts` in `mod LeakyDet` (declared `cap deterministic`) calls `unix_time_ms`, which is non-deterministic.\n\nUse deterministic operations only in a `cap deterministic` module. Avoid random/time builtins.","code":"deterministic_violation","fix":null,"labels":[],"notes":[],"category":"CONTRACT"}
